ustralian and us sunscreens  that really matters  apart from the newer filters is the  broad spectrum label  this tells you about uva protection  which i go through in my uva video but  very briefly  in the us this means it passes one  criteria called the critical wavelength  so at least 10 of the area under the uv  absorption curve has to be at 370  nanometers or higher  that means there's going to be a bit  more uva protection but in australia  it's stricter  so on top of this there's also the  requirement that uva protection has to  be at least one-third of the labelled  spf  a lot of sunscreens that are considered  broad spectrum in the us aren't actually  broad spectrum in australia europe is  the same as australia for this  instead of broad spectrum they use the  uva circle logo everyone is obsessed  with the bondi sand sunscreen at the  moment  it has no white cast it's a little bit  sticky on my skin but it's fine once i  powder over it  but it is a lot lighter than a lot of us  sunscreens i've tried it's also four  hours water resistant which is a big  bonus  there's a face on a body version and  from the ingredients they look like  they're the same formula but i haven't  actually tried a tube that's labeled  face yet to confirm  bonded sands are also releasing a whole  bunch of new sunscreens which i'm  excited to try everyday humans is a new  brand for me this is resting beach face  it's spf 30 which is great for everyday  use is really nice and light  i should also point out that there are a  lot of international brands that are  approved in australia so they also pass  australian standards  i think we sometimes forget about this  so this includes sunscreens from like  neutrogena la roche posay  so you might already be overlooking some  higher quality sunscreens that meet  australian standards  now for some zinc sunscreens i don't  hate i don't think this is a surprise to  anyone but zinc oxide and i do not get  along i can always feel them on my skin  they tend to be thick and clog up my  pores and mess up my fringe  sometimes they dehydrate my skin and  make it go flaky while also feeling  greasy which is like top 10 skincare  betrayals there's usually white cast  they just don't spark joy but i found a  few that i actually quite like and this  is a new and exciting experience for me  it's ultraviolet lean screen  paula's choice super light daily wrinkle  defense and naked sunday's  collagen glow mineral sunscreen i think  a lot of brands have caught on to the  fact that if you put a bit of tint into  a zinc oxide sunscreen  then it reduces white cast even if that  tint doesn't necessarily match  people's skin tone all three of these  are pretty much invisible on my skin  even though they all look like different  colors coming out of the tube but i've  still seen people with darker skin  complain about white cast from the  ultraviolet and the puller's choice i  expect it'll be the same for the naked  sundays since it's even lighter in tint  than the other two paula's choice isn't  actually approved in australia as a  proper sunscreen  but it is really nice and light in  texture which is amazing for a zinc  oxide sunscreen the other two are spf 50  plus which means they have more than  double the protection yeah spf 50 plus  means  spf 60 or higher in australia things  work differently here there was like a  whole emu war  anyway these two are a fair bit thicker  in texture they're a little bit like  paste  i have to powder quite thickly on top to  make sure my fringe doesn't stick down  on them but  if i had to wear a really high  protection zinc oxide sunscreen it would  be one of these two  this is the face version of every  sunscreen evie are a swedish brand and  they use the special technology that  gets the sunscreen into the top layers  of skin so it doesn't rub off easily  it's very cool i've talked about the  data that backs up their claims before  but this felt a little bit sticky and  uncomfortable on my face apparently with  swedish customers  this is considered really light and  comfortable it might be because i've  been sport with these light  asian and australian sunscreens or it  might be because it's so humid in sydney  i still use evie a lot on my body though  so that is it those are my favorites let  me know if you've tried any of these  and what you thought or if you have  sunscreens that you recommend that i  haven't mentioned before like subscribe  and follow let me know what other  categories of products you want me to go  through my favorites of next time there  is probably a 50 50 chance i'll be  talking about sunscreen again  you
 

